The program is a 55-line counter: validate [38;5;10margc == 2[0m, [38;5;10mstrtol(argv[1], &end, 10)[0m, reject if nothing parsed, then print [38;5;10mval[0m and increment until [38;5;10mval % 10 == 9[0m. I did the translation myself rather than delegating — it was small enough that a sub-agent round trip plus verification would cost more than writing it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three subtleties I reproduced rather than "fixed":[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mint val = strtol(...)[0m narrows a 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m to [38;5;10mint[0m. So [38;5;10m9223372036854775807[0m parses to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m, truncates to [38;5;10m-1[0m, and the program prints [38;5;10m-1 … 9[0m. I hand-rolled [38;5;10mstrtol[0m (C-locale whitespace skip, optional sign, digits, saturation to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, [38;5;10mend == nptr[0m on no conversion) instead of using Rust's [38;5;10mparse[0m, which would reject partial input like [38;5;10m12abc[0m and error on overflow.[0m[0m
- C's [38;5;10m%[0m truncates toward zero, so [38;5;10mval % 10 == 9[0m is never true for negatives; negative starts count up through [38;5;10m9[0m.[0m[0m
- Incrementing past [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m is signed-overflow UB in C. I checked what the reference binary actually does: gcc -O2 wraps to [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m and keeps counting (~2.1 billion lines), so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m. Verified with matching 300 MB output prefixes for [38;5;10m2147483647[0m, [38;5;10m2147483640[0m, and [38;5;10m-2147483648[0m.[0m[0m
